{"records":[{"id":"65pfcj4j4m","title":"Protocol of CRISPR-Cas9 knockout screens for identifying ferroptosis regulators","description":"Ferroptosis, an iron-dependent programmed cell death triggered by excessive lipid peroxidation, has shown promising therapeutic potentials in human diseases. Here, we describe a protocol of a CRISPR-Cas9 loss-of-function screen to identify regulators in response to different inducers of ferroptosis. We emphasize the steps of library amplification, drug treatment, high-throughput sequencing preparation, and bioinformatics analysis using Model-based Analysis of Genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 Knockout (MAGeCK). We also present a method to discover the regulators of ferroptosis and verify the potential targets efficiently.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-10-24T01:46:48.793Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/65pfcj4j4m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/65pfcj4j4m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xin","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wei","last_name":"Gu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR/Cas9","Ferroptosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rhsw3nbjhz","title":"Essential gene profiles for human pluripotent stem cells identify uncharacterized genes and substrate dependencies. Mair et al.","description":"Human pluripotent stem cells (hPSCs) provide an invaluable tool for modeling diseases and hold promise for regenerative medicine. For understanding pluripotency and lineage differentiation mechanisms, a critical first step involves systematically cataloging genes that are indispensable for hPSC maintenance and proliferation. To map genetic determinants of hPSC fitness, we performed genome-scale loss-of-function screens in an inducible Cas9 H1 hPSC line cultured on feeder cells and feeder-free to identify essential genes. Among these, we found FOXH1 and VENTX, genes that encode transcription factors previously implicated in stem cell biology, as well as an uncharacterized gene, C22orf43/DRICH1. hPSCs essential genes are substantially different from other cell lines, and gene essentiality in hPSCs is highly context-dependent with respect to different growth substrates. Our CRISPR screens establish parameters for genome-wide screens in hPSCs, which will facilitate the characterization of unappreciated regulators of hPSC biology to understand the genetic wiring of hPSC fitness and pluripotency.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-01-24T11:57:50.932Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rhsw3nbjhz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rhsw3nbjhz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Barbara","last_name":"Mair","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jelena","last_name":"Tomic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sanna","last_name":"Masud","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jason","last_name":"Moffat","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Stem Cell","Functional Genomics","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"z4ht3txn2k","title":"Accompanying Files for paper entitled: \"Genome-scale mapping of DNA damage suppressors through phenotypic CRISPR/Cas9 screens\" - Zhao et al. Molecular Cell 2023","description":"Accompanying Files for paper entitled: \"Genome-scale mapping of DNA damage suppressors through phenotypic CRISPR/Cas9 screens\" - Zhao et al. Molecular Cell 2023. This dataset contains Source and additional Data for all figures.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-06-06T07:16:19.286Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/z4ht3txn2k","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/z4ht3txn2k"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Durocher","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yichao","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01gavpb45","name":"Canadian Institutes of Health Research","grant_id":null}],"categories":["DNA Replication","DNA Repair","Flow Cytometry","Cancer Chemotherapy","DNA Damage","Functional Genomics","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"f95mdjrwcy","title":"ADC Downstream Purification Technology","description":"Antibody-drug conjugate (ADC) is a complex that conjugates cytotoxin to monoclonal antibodies through a constructed linker and selectively delivers cytotoxins to tumors. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-07-19T19:27:56.921Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/f95mdjrwcy","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/f95mdjrwcy"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Linna","last_name":"Green","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Chemistry","Biotechnology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gfcn2wmrpf","title":"Dataset for the paper entitled \"A genetic map of the response to DNA damage in human cells\" by Olivieri M et al. Cell 2020","description":"We used pooled CRISPR/Cas9 screens in the human RPE1-hTERT p53-/- cell line against 27 genotoxic agents. The Dataset herein make the primary data available. The files are:\n\n* Additional genes of interest (discussion and data relating to TMEM2, ESD, USP37, PHF12, BTAF1 and DRAP1)\n* Supplementary Raw Data: Data used to make all the graphs in the manuscript\n* Folder \"CRISPR screen readcount files\". Readcounts for the screens undertaken as part of this study.\n* Folder \"Raw images for immunoblots\": uncropped images for all the blots in the manuscript\n* Folder: \"Additional QC analyses of CRISPR screens\": Additional analyses that measure the quality of the screens using Presion-Recall curves of essential genes and an estimate of genes that scored as hits due to batch effects.\n* Folder \"Data_Analysis_Code\" R markdown file along with data files\n* File: \"Additional Genes of Interests\" contains a short discussion of genes we found noteworthy after analysis of our screen data.\n\nVersion 2 note: this version differs from Version 1 by a single file (FDRPos_31screens.csv) which has a tab error fixed.\n\n\n\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-07-07T14:26:40.176Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gfcn2wmrpf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gfcn2wmrpf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Durocher","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michele","last_name":"Olivieri","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Data Quality","Primary Data"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"6bkjdg5g85","title":"Zmat3 is a key splicing regulator in the p53 tumor suppression program","description":"Although TP53 is the most commonly mutated gene in human cancers, the p53-dependent transcriptional programs mediating tumor suppression remain incompletely understood. Here, to uncover critical components downstream of p53 in tumor suppression, we perform unbiased RNAi and CRISPR/Cas9-based genetic screens in vivo.  These screens converge upon the p53-inducible gene Zmat3, encoding an RNA-binding-protein, and we demonstrate that ZMAT3 is an important tumor suppressor downstream of p53 in mouse KrasG12D-driven lung and liver cancers and human carcinomas. Integrative analysis of the ZMAT3 RNA-binding landscape and transcriptomic profiling reveals that ZMAT3 directly modulates exon inclusion in transcripts encoding proteins of diverse functions, including the p53 inhibitors MDM4 and MDM2, splicing regulators, and components of varied cellular processes. Interestingly, these exons are enriched in NMD signals, and, accordingly, ZMAT3 broadly affects target transcript stability. Collectively, these studies reveal ZMAT3 as a novel RNA-splicing and homeostasis regulator and key component of p53-mediated tumor suppression.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-11-06T16:30:19.918Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6bkjdg5g85","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6bkjdg5g85"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kathryn","last_name":"Bieging-Rolett","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Tumor","Alternative Splicing","RNA Sequencing","P53","Mouse Model of Cancer","Adenocarcinoma","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"4g722x4jfg","title":"Western blot","description":"Western blot of CRISPR-Cas9-mediated nontargeting (NT) versus BACH2 knockout 3 days after CRISPR-Cas9-gRNA nucleofection of human gut cells.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-07-02T21:20:55.966Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4g722x4jfg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4g722x4jfg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yulong","last_name":"Wei","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Gut","Western Blot","T Cell","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"r49yg49ddc","title":"Genetic screens identify host factors for SARS-CoV-2 and common cold coronaviruses. Wang et al.","description":"Supplemental Tables S1 to S5 in Microsoft Excel format for the article \"Genetic screens identify host factors for SARS-CoV-2 and common cold coronaviruses\" (Wang et al., 2020):\nTable S1: CRISPR screen results. MaGECK output for positive gene enrichment analysis of SARS-CoV-2, 229E and OC43 host factor screens. Related to Figure 1. \nTable S2: Gene ontology enrichment analysis of individual CRISPR screens and network propagation clusters. Related to Figure 2.\nTable S3: Network propagation results. Related to Figure 2. \nTable S4: RNP editing efficiencies in A549-ACE2 and Huh7.5.1-ACE2/TMPRSS2 cells. Related to Figures 3 and 5.\nTable S5: Oligonucleotide sequences used in this study. Related to STAR Methods. \n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-11-25T18:59:20.104Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/r49yg49ddc","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/r49yg49ddc"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Andreas","last_name":"Puschnik","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Functional Genomics","Coronavirus","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"n5j5pmbhrm","title":"Supplementary Table. Components, mechanisms of action, and clinical indications of FDA-approved ADCs","description":"Indications obtained from FDA package inserts. \nADC = Antibody-drug conjugate; AML = acute myelogenous leukemia; ALCL = anaplastic large cell lymphoma; ALL = acute lymphoblastic leukemia; DLBCL = diffuse large B cell lymphoma; DM4 = maytansine; FDA = Food and Drug Administration; FRα = folate receptor α; HR = hormone receptor; HER2 = human epidermal growth factor receptor 2; IgG = immunoglobulin; mAb = monoclonal antibody; MF = mycosis fungoides; MMAE = monomethyl auristatin E; NSCLC = non-small-cell lung cancer; PD-(L)1 = programmed cell death protein (Ligand) 1; TF = tissue factor; TROP-2 = trophoblastic antigen 2","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-03-15T14:30:38.515Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/n5j5pmbhrm","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/n5j5pmbhrm"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Christian","last_name":"Gronbeck","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Dermatology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"f5jtt3s8hv","title":"GCN2 is a determinant of the response to WEE1 kinase inhibition in small-cell lung cancer ","description":"Patients with small-cell lung cancer (SCLC) are in dire need of more effective therapeutic options. Frequent disruption of the G1 checkpoint in SCLC cells creates a dependency on the G2/M checkpoint to maintain genomic integrity. Indeed, in pre-clinical models, inhibiting the G2/M checkpoint kinase WEE1 shows promise in inhibiting SCLC growth. However, toxicity and acquired resistance have limited the clinical effectiveness of this strategy. Here, using CRISPR/Cas9 knockout screens in vitro and in vivo, we identified multiple factors influencing the response of SCLC cells to the WEE1 kinase inhibitor AZD1775, including the GCN2 kinase and other members of its signaling pathway. Rapid activation of GCN2 upon AZD1775 treatment triggers a stress response in SCLC cells. Pharmacological or genetic activation of the GCN2 pathway enhances cancer cell killing by AZD1775. Thus, activation of the GCN2 pathway represents a novel approach for augmenting the efficacy of WEE1 inhibitors in SCLC.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-06-26T09:21:15.032Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/f5jtt3s8hv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/f5jtt3s8hv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Alexander","last_name":"Drainas","orcid":"0000-0001-9081-9850"},{"first_name":"Julien","last_name":"Sage","orcid":"0000-0002-8928-9968"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology","Flow Cytometry","Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":121431,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":131,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":121431,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":120982,"restricted":60,"embargoed":389},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2037},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1324},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":551},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":441},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":416},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":379},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":379},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":338},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":273},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":245}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":118867},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1889},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":174},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":126},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":70},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":58},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":52},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":38},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":30},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":865},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":749},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":399},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":384},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":357}]}}